WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Tuesday,

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.440
the twentieth of October.

3
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.320
This episode is written with AI,
and read by an AI clone of my voice.

4
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.400
Iran's main oil terminals have sat
empty for weeks under the US naval

5
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.173
blockade, while Iraq says its exports
are almost back to pre-war levels.

6
00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:22.720
And Washington says it will let
sanctioned dark-fleet tankers be sold

7
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.840
back into legitimate trade, just
as tanker earnings hit records.

8
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.800
On the markets. Brent came within a
cent of one hundred and ten dollars

9
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.640
overnight, then eased through Asian
and European trading as signs of

10
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.720
recovering Middle East
supply offset fresh attacks,

11
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.640
according to OilPrice dot com.

12
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.360
On Friday, after US Central Command
confirmed that a projectile from

13
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.120
Iran's Revolutionary Guard Corps had
sunk a Singapore-flagged container

14
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.800
ship in the strait, December Brent
rose to one hundred and nine dollars

15
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.880
fifty. November West Texas Intermediate
reached one hundred and one dollars

16
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.280
thirty, its first time in
triple digits for a month.

17
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.080
In Europe, gas storage stood at
seventy-four point six percent full on

18
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.240
the eighteenth, according to
Gas Infrastructure Europe.

19
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.240
Iran's main oil-loading terminals
have had no ships calling for weeks,

20
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.200
gCaptain reported. It's a clear sign
the blockade is choking Tehran's

21
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.400
exports.

22
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.920
Next door, Iraq is filling the gap.

23
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.640
Its oil minister told the Iraqi News
Agency that October exports have

24
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.453
averaged more than three point
four million barrels a day so far.

25
00:01:24.507 --> 00:01:27.680
That's close to the three point three
to three point five million shipped

26
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.440
before the war. It's also about seven
hundred and fifty thousand barrels

27
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.400
a day more than in September.

28
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.520
Of September's two point six
five million barrels a day,

29
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.680
two hundred and fifty thousand went
through the Iraq-Turkey pipeline

30
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.907
to Ceyhan. The October
number is the ministry's own,

31
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:45.000
and it hasn't been independently checked.

32
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.120
If it holds, Gulf medium-sour supply
is moving from Iranian to Iraqi

33
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.400
barrels rather than simply shrinking.

34
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.560
That could ease things a little for
sour-heavy refiners in India and

35
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.880
across Asia.

36
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.680
Over in shipping. Last week Washington
was only weighing relief for the

37
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.600
shadow fleet. Now Energy Secretary
Chris Wright says the administration

38
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.200
is authorising the sale of sanctioned
tankers so they can carry crude

39
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.600
legitimately, according to gCaptain.

40
00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.120
That account comes from Wright alone,
and the reports carry no Treasury

41
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.840
terms or vessel numbers.

42
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.120
For charterers, the timing is the point.

43
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.960
Broker Hartland estimates independent
tanker owners are earning about

44
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.080
four hundred million dollars a day
more than they originally expected.

45
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.840
Bring enough of those ships
into the compliant fleet,

46
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.400
and freight could come off those extremes.

47
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.120
The strain isn't limited to crude.

48
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.960
Kpler reports that rising attacks in
the Strait of Hormuz are hampering

49
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.880
the growing Gulf shuttle trade
in liquefied petroleum gas.

50
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.880
Only the headline is out
so far, with no figures.

51
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.520
Turning to Russia. Late
on Friday the sixteenth,

52
00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:51.280
President Trump signed an executive
order waiving the new Russia sanctions

53
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.760
required under the Graham legislation.

54
00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.040
Kyiv and some US lawmakers
have criticised the move,

55
00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.200
OilPrice dot com reported.

56
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.880
Trump says the waiver and the diesel
export deal are both meant to keep

57
00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.000
Putin at the peace talks,
according to Rigzone.

58
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.840
An OilPrice dot com commentary puts
the deal at three hundred thousand

59
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.640
tonnes of Russian diesel
released to the US.

60
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.520
Neither government has
confirmed that figure.

61
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.880
Russia's Arctic route,
meanwhile, is getting busier.

62
00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.160
This season, about twenty million
barrels of Russian crude have gone

63
00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.400
to Asia via the Northern Sea Route,
according to traders and data cited

64
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.027
by gCaptain. That count
runs only to mid-October,

65
00:03:30.053 --> 00:03:34.560
and it's already nearly fifty percent
above last year's full season.

66
00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.720
In gas, a Qatari liquefied natural
gas carrier is heading north through

67
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.587
the Red Sea toward European
buyers, gCaptain reported.

68
00:03:42.613 --> 00:03:46.347
It's a rare transit. The shorter
route could cut delivery times,

69
00:03:46.373 --> 00:03:49.720
but it puts the cargo back
within reach of Houthi attacks.

70
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.280
If more cargoes follow, part of
Europe's winter supply would hang on

71
00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.480
the Bab el-Mandeb strait.

72
00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:56.907
A few more things.

73
00:03:56.933 --> 00:04:01.200
Wright also told ABC that US gasoline
and diesel prices peaked a few

74
00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.613
weeks ago. That's his
view, not a data release.

75
00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:08.320
The EIA's latest short-term outlook
trimmed its forecast of this year's

76
00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.800
supply crunch, though the
reports don't give the numbers.

77
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.440
About a quarter of the world's sulfur
supply passed through Hormuz before

78
00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.360
the war. Middle East sulfur has climbed
from around five hundred dollars

79
00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.160
a tonne to above eight hundred,
according to OilPrice dot com.

80
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.040
That leaves fertiliser makers who
rely on sulfur from refineries paying

81
00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.720
far more.

82
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.640
Pakistan's economy grew just two
percent in the quarter to June as Qatari

83
00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.000
gas deliveries turned irregular,
according to the Pakistan Bureau of

84
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.560
Statistics. And Statistics Canada
says inflation reached three point

85
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.080
one percent in September.

86
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.400
Fuel oil prices there were up nearly
fifty-eight percent on a year earlier.

87
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.520
In Norway, Aker BP's Skarv satellite
fields have started up a year ahead

88
00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:55.520
of plan. They hold about one hundred
and twenty million barrels of oil

89
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.840
equivalent. And the North American
rig count fell by five over the week,

90
00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:01.440
according to Baker Hughes.

91
00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:05.800
What to watch next. Tomorrow brings
the EIA weekly for the week to the

92
00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.600
sixteenth, the first read on distillate
stocks since the diesel deal.

93
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.000
Europe's daily storage readings keep
coming through to the twenty-seventh.

94
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.000
And the shut-in figures for Hurricane
Isaias from US offshore regulators

95
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.200
were due on the sixteenth
and still haven't appeared.

96
00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.880
That's the brief. Sources for everything
